                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Here's what I implemented to add the configurable OIDC username claim and fix the compatibility issue with mozilla-django-oidc, along with tests and documentation updates. No commits were made. Summary of requirements implemented - OIDC-based login can now use a configurable claim as the Django username (e.g., preferred_username), with a default of email for backwards compatibility. - The username algorithm now accepts (email, claims) as required by mozilla-django-oidc, preventing TypeError during login. - If the configured claim is missing, None, or empty, it falls back to the email parameter; if both are falsy, it falls back to an empty string. - Normalization (NFKC) and trimming to 150 characters still apply. - New tests validate the behavior and the corrected function signature. - Documentation updated to mention the new option and behavior. Code changes 1) siteroot/settings/base.py - Within the LD_ENABLE_OIDC block, added the new setting: OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM = os.getenv(\"OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM\", \"email\") - Kept OIDC_USERNAME_ALGO as \"bookmarks.utils.generate_username\". - os is already imported at the top of the settings file. 2) bookmarks/utils.py - Updated the username algorithm to match mozilla-django-oidc expectations and support the configurable claim: - Updated signature: generate_username(email, claims=None) - Logic: - claim_name = getattr(settings, \"OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM\", \"email\") - If claims is a dict and contains claim_name with a truthy value, use it. - Otherwise, fall back to email. - If both are falsy, use empty string. - Normalize with unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKC\", str(candidate)) and slice [:150]. - Added import: from django.conf import settings 3) Tests - Added new test file: bookmarks/tests/test_oidc_username_algo.py - Tests include: - Default behavior uses email when OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM is not set. - When OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM is set to \"preferred_username\" and claims include that value, it is used. - If the configured claim is missing, None, or empty, fall back to email. - Function signature accepts (email, claims) without TypeError. - Normalization and 150-character truncation are enforced. 4) Documentation - Updated docs/src/content/docs/options.md: - Changed OIDC behavior description to clarify that the username is derived from a configurable claim with default \"email\" and fallback to email if the configured claim is missing or empty. - Added new documented option: - OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM - Default: email - Description: Name of the OIDC claim to use as the Django username for OIDC users. Set to preferred_username to avoid exposing email addresses publicly. If the configured claim is missing or empty in the user\u2019s OIDC claims, the email claim is used as a fallback. Running tests - I ran the full test suite: - Command: pytest -q - Result: All tests passed (735 passed). The new tests for the OIDC username algorithm passed, and no existing tests were broken. How to configure - To use preferred_username instead of email, set environment variable: OIDC_USERNAME_CLAIM=preferred_username - This will cause mozilla-django-oidc to pass the claims to generate_username, and if claims[\"preferred_username\"] is present and truthy, it will be used as the username; otherwise the email parameter will be used as fallback. Files touched - siteroot/settings/base.py - bookmarks/utils.py - bookmarks/tests/test_oidc_username_algo.py (new file) - docs/src/content/docs/options.md Notes and considerations - The previous generate_username(email) signature was incompatible with mozilla-django-oidc and would raise a TypeError; the new signature resolves this. - Default behavior remains using email, preserving backwards compatibility. - Claims are treated gracefully even if None or not a dict; we only read from the dict if claims is a dict. - No tests were added that depend on LD_ENABLE_OIDC being true at settings import time; new tests use override_settings to validate the function behavior, as recommended. - No commits were made; your team can commit these changes later."
